1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:20,950
Ancient Chinese paintings can be traced back to as early as 5,000 to 6,000 years ago, when people began to use minerals to draw simple pictures resembling animals, plants, and even human beings on rocks and produce drawings of amazing designs and decorations on the surface of pottery and later bronze containers.
中国古代绘画最早可以追溯到5000至6000年前：当时，人们开始用矿物在岩石上画出形似动物、植物乃至人的简单图画，并在陶器以及后来的青铜器表面绘出图案和纹饰都令人惊叹的画作。

2
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:24,890
However, only a few of the works have survived over time.
然而，这些作品中只有少数历经岁月保存了下来。

3
00:00:25,690 --> 00:00:34,740
The earliest drawings that have been preserved till today were produced on paper and silk, which were burial articles with a history of over 2,000 years.
保存至今最早的画作是画在纸和绢上的，它们都是随葬品，已有2000多年的历史。

4
00:00:35,540 --> 00:00:44,750
As far as the subject is concerned, Chinese paintings fall into several categories, such as figure paintings, landscapes, and flower-and-bird paintings.
就题材而言，中国画分为若干门类，如人物画、山水画和花鸟画。

5
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:55,240
European paintings, introduced into China in the 17th century, were called “Western paintings,” and the traditional local works, “the Chinese paintings.”
17世纪传入中国的欧洲绘画被称为“西洋画”，而本土的传统作品则被称为“中国画”。

6
00:00:56,040 --> 00:01:04,670
As the representative of Eastern paintings, Chinese paintings greatly differ from the Western counterpart in terms of contents, forms, and styles.
作为东方绘画的代表，中国画在内容、形式和风格上都与西方绘画大不相同。

7
00:01:05,470 --> 00:01:22,730
In terms of drawing skills, Chinese paintings can be categorized into two styles: colored paintings and water-ink paintings, with the former dominant before the 12th century by professional or craftsman painters, and the latter in and after the 12th century by literati painters.
就绘画技法而言，中国画可分为两种风格：设色画和水墨画；前者在12世纪以前占主导地位，以职业画家或工匠画家为主，后者则自12世纪起占主导地位，以文人画家为主。

8
00:01:23,530 --> 00:01:30,230
Also known as “fine-stroke” paintings, colored paintings feature close attention to details and fine brushwork.
设色画又称“工笔”画，其特点是注重细节、笔法精细。

9
00:01:31,030 --> 00:01:38,790
Thanks to the mineral-made dyes, the original colors can be fully maintained and the paintings will not fade away as time goes by.
由于采用矿物制成的颜料，原有的色彩得以完整保留，画作也不会随着时间推移而褪色。

10
00:01:39,590 --> 00:01:48,190
Colored paintings, which manifest in themselves an unparalleled graceful air, were widely welcomed among the painters serving in royal courts.
设色画本身就展现出无与伦比的典雅气韵，在供职于宫廷的画家中广受欢迎。

11
00:01:48,990 --> 00:02:02,300
In contrast, water-ink paintings, also called “thick-stroke” paintings, are supposed to convey spiritual resonance with strokes as simple as possible, instead of attaching much importance to the realistic subjects.
与之相反，水墨画又称“写意”画，讲究以尽可能简练的笔触传达神韵，而不太看重写实的题材。

12
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:12,280
Exaggerated forms, such as generalization and hyperbole as well as rich imagination, are employed in painting to display painters’ feelings.
作画时，画家会运用夸张的表现形式，如概括、夸张手法以及丰富的想象，来抒发自己的情感。

13
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:17,290
Therefore, it is relatively difficult to make a copy of a water-ink painting.
因此，临摹水墨画相对较难。

14
00:02:18,090 --> 00:02:21,290
However, there is no absolute line between the two schools.
然而，两派之间并没有绝对的界限。

15
00:02:22,090 --> 00:02:31,600
No matter which school they belonged to, painters could and did compromise a little and learn from each other, giving rise to a mixed style including elements from both.
无论属于哪一派，画家们都能够、也确实会稍作折中，相互借鉴，从而形成了兼具两派元素的混合风格。

16
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:40,890
Traditional Chinese paintings perfectly integrate poetry, calligraphy, painting, and seal engraving, all of which are necessary components.
传统中国画将诗、书、画、印完美地融为一体，四者都是必不可少的组成部分。

17
00:02:41,690 --> 00:02:57,970
As Chinese is an ideographic language system, most of the Chinese characters in ancient times were produced with concise strokes according to the shapes or meanings of the signified, which naturally generated an artistic form of calligraphy closely connected with painting.
由于汉语是一种表意文字体系，古代的大多数汉字都是依据所指之物的形状或意义、用简练的笔画造出来的，这自然而然地催生了与绘画紧密相连的书法艺术形式。

18
00:02:58,770 --> 00:03:05,410
Chinese painters, in particular literati painters, would unconsciously draw with the styles of calligraphy.
中国画家，尤其是文人画家，会不自觉地以书法的笔法作画。

19
00:03:06,210 --> 00:03:11,520
Writing poems became an established way to express their feelings during the process of drawing.
在作画过程中，题诗成了他们抒发情感的一种固定方式。

20
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:22,570
In the end, according to the tradition of Chinese literati, red seals with their names engraved were used to sign their works, a tradition that continues to this day.
最后，按照中国文人的传统，他们会用刻有自己名字的红色印章在作品上落款，这一传统一直延续至今。

21
00:03:23,370 --> 00:03:27,660
Chinese paintings clearly reveal that the Chinese think in a holistic way.
中国画清楚地表明，中国人是从整体上思考问题的。

22
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:37,850
Before drawing, painters must have an overall plan concerning the content of poems, the style of calligraphy, and the place where the works should be signed.
作画之前，画家必须对诗的内容、书法的风格以及作品落款的位置有一个通盘的规划。

23
00:03:38,650 --> 00:03:49,040
The criterion of “Painting in poetry and poetry in painting” for excellent works was originally set by Su Shi, the well-known painter and writer in the Northern Song Dynasty.
优秀作品要“诗中有画，画中有诗”，这一标准最初是由北宋著名画家、文学家苏轼确立的。

24
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:52,960
Other painters later held this idea in high esteem.
后来，其他画家也十分推崇这一观点。

25
00:03:53,760 --> 00:04:10,660
An obvious distinction between Chinese and European paintings lies in the fact that a piece of blank space is always reserved in Chinese paintings for clouds over mountains, fog haunting above rivers, light circles reflected from the sun or the moon, or nothing at all.
中国画与欧洲绘画的一个明显区别在于，中国画中总会留出一片空白，用来表现山间的云、河上缭绕的雾、日月映出的光晕，或者什么也不表现。

26
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:20,460
Some argue that the “blankness” in Chinese paintings is the most appropriate vehicle to convey the uncertainty and ambiguity featured in Chinese poems.
有人认为，中国画中的“留白”是传达中国诗歌所特有的不确定性和模糊性最恰当的载体。

27
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:33,820
Ample evidence can be found in fine-art archeology that paintings with people as the subject turned out to be the earliest one among all the categories of Chinese paintings and used to enjoy prosperous development.
美术考古中有充分的证据表明，以人物为题材的绘画是中国画所有门类中最早出现的一类，并且曾经繁荣发展。

28
00:04:34,620 --> 00:04:44,060
Dating back to the primitive age, ancestors drew pictures of human beings and animals on walls and rocks with white chalks, red bauxite, or charcoal.
早在原始时代，先民们就用白垩、红铝土或木炭在墙壁和岩石上画人和动物。

29
00:04:44,860 --> 00:05:00,920
However, figure paintings didn’t get fully developed until 1,500 years ago, when Gu Kaizhi, the famous painter and art theorist, asserted that more attention should be paid not only to the external shapes of figures, but also the internal quality.
然而，人物画直到1500年前才得到充分发展。当时，著名画家、艺术理论家顾恺之主张，应当更加重视的不仅是人物的外在形体，还有其内在品质。

30
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:09,940
This argument was accepted by artists and critics and taken as an established rule guiding the future production and comments of similar works.
这一主张为艺术家和评论家所接受，并被奉为指导后世同类作品创作与评论的既定准则。

31
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:27,620
As the most important and most influential category among Chinese paintings, the landscape paintings, which came into being much later and developed quickly in the Tang Dynasty, usually take images of natural scenery, such as mountains or rivers, as the subjects.
山水画是中国画中最重要、最有影响力的门类，它出现得要晚得多，在唐代迅速发展，通常以山川等自然风景为题材。

32
00:05:28,420 --> 00:05:34,710
Over the long history of paintings in ancient China, the largest portion of painters specialized in landscapes.
在中国古代漫长的绘画史上，专攻山水画的画家人数最多。

33
00:05:35,510 --> 00:05:45,080
Landscape painting could reach its heyday and maintain its dominant role in Chinese paintings, primarily because of its deep root in Chinese traditional culture.
山水画之所以能达到鼎盛并在中国画中保持主导地位，主要是因为它深深植根于中国传统文化。

34
00:05:45,880 --> 00:05:56,900
Chinese philosophers in ancient times believed in the “unity of nature and man,” which means that human beings can feel nature and therefore should be in a harmonious relationship with it.
中国古代哲学家信奉“天人合一”，即人能够感知自然，因而应当与自然和谐相处。

35
00:05:57,700 --> 00:06:09,700
Having widely accepted this argument, Chinese painters tended to integrate their feelings and dreams into the natural scenery they were producing, while arousing aspiration and imagination from readers.
中国画家广泛接受了这一主张，往往将自己的情感与梦想融入所描绘的自然风景之中，同时激发观者的向往与想象。

36
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:20,850
With flowers and grass, bamboos and rocks, birds and beasts, and worms and fish as the major subjects, flower-and-bird paintings are usually viewed and admired by viewers.
花鸟画以花草、竹石、鸟兽、虫鱼为主要题材，通常供观者观赏品鉴。

37
00:06:21,650 --> 00:06:27,930
The most frequently seen pictures feature a combination of flowers and birds, hence the flower-and-bird name.
最常见的画作以花与鸟的组合为特色，“花鸟画”之名即由此而来。

38
00:06:28,730 --> 00:06:36,920
Originally designed for craftwork, flower-and-bird painting eventually established itself as an independent school in the Tang Dynasty.
花鸟画最初是为工艺品而设计的，最终在唐代确立为一个独立的画派。

39
00:06:37,720 --> 00:06:50,780
Considered sublime and elegant by literati in ancient China, the so-called “four gentlemen” including plum blossom, orchid, bamboo, and chrysanthemum are the most welcomed subjects in Chinese flower-and-bird paintings.
中国古代文人认为它们高洁典雅，所谓的“四君子”，即梅、兰、竹、菊，是中国花鸟画中最受欢迎的题材。
